      "body": "### Purpose\n\nFix an issue where 0-argument tool calls in `/v1/messages` streaming mode are misclassified with `stop_reason: \"stop_sequence\"` or `\"end_turn\"` instead of `\"tool_use\"`.\n\n### Problem\n\nIn `vllm/entrypoints/anthropic/serving.py`, `_to_anthropic_stop_reason` determines whether a tool call is complete using:\n```python\ntool_complete = (tool_used and _is_json(\"\".join(last_tool_args)))\n```\nWhen a model invokes a function that takes no parameters (0-argument tool call), the streamed arguments are empty (`\"\"`). Because `json.loads(\"\")` raises `json.JSONDecodeError`, `_is_json(\"\")` returned `False`.\n\nAs a result, `tool_complete` evaluated to `False`. When the model matched a stop token (e.g. `</tool_call>`), `_to_anthropic_stop_reason` returned `(\"stop_sequence\", \"</tool_call>\")` or `\"end_turn\"` instead of `\"tool_use\"`. Anthropic API clients (such as Claude Code, Aider, Cursor) require `stop_reason == \"tool_use\"` to trigger tool execution and would drop or fail the tool call.\n\n### Solution\n\n- In `_is_json`, strip whitespace and treat empty strings as valid complete JSON payloads (`True`).\n- Add unit test coverage in `tests/entrypoints/anthropic/test_anthropic_messages_conversion.py` testing `_is_json` with empty/whitespace strings and verifying that 0-arg completed tool calls report `stop_reason=\"tool_use\"`.",
